{"absolutePath":"notes/coverage/poc/Tiny2.sol","exportedSymbols":{"Tiny2":[61]},"id":62,"license":"MIT","nodeType":"SourceUnit","nodes":[{"id":1,"literals":["solidity","^","0.8",".20"],"nodeType":"PragmaDirective","src":"32:24:0"},{"abstract":false,"baseContracts":[],"canonicalName":"Tiny2","contractDependencies":[],"contractKind":"contract","documentation":{"id":2,"nodeType":"StructuredDocumentation","src":"58:1070:0","text":"DISCRIMINATING EXPERIMENT. Identical to `Tiny` except that the constructor\n puts the balance in place, so `withdraw`'s guarded paths need NO preceding\n call in the transaction.\n   Tiny  : `bal` starts 0; only `deposit` can raise it.\n   Tiny2 : `bal` starts 500; `withdraw`'s guard is satisfiable at entry.\n Tiny measured: `--focus-function withdraw` gives 5 paths, 3 F, 2\n bounded-holds; and dropping the focus entirely gives 8 paths, 6 F, THE SAME\n 2 bounded-holds. So a whole-contract dispatcher, which may call `deposit()`\n and then `withdraw()` inside one transaction, did not witness them either.\n If Tiny2's two guarded paths come back F, the blocker is not the STATE but\n the PRECEDING CALL -- i.e. the path identity `tr != enc || cnt != depth`\n excludes exactly the executions that would establish the state, because a\n preceding call contributes its own decisions to the same accumulator.\n If they come back bounded-holds here too, the accumulator is innocent and\n something else refuses the guard."},"fullyImplemented":true,"id":61,"linearizedBaseContracts":[61],"name":"Tiny2","nameLocation":"1137:5:0","nodeType":"ContractDefinition","nodes":[{"constant":false,"functionSelector":"3d79d1c8","id":4,"mutability":"mutable","name":"bal","nameLocation":"1164:3:0","nodeType":"VariableDeclaration","scope":61,"src":"1149:18:0","stateVariable":true,"storageLocation":"default","typeDescriptions":{"typeIdentifier":"t_uint256","typeString":"uint256"},"typeName":{"id":3,"name":"uint256","nodeType":"ElementaryTypeName","src":"1149:7:0","typeDescripti